1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,222
Quick question.

2
00:00:02,382 --> 00:00:06,409
Your server is in Virginia. Your shopper is in Mumbai. How long

3
00:00:06,409 --> 00:00:09,030
does one product photo take to reach her?

4
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:11,062
Longer than you think.

5
00:00:11,222 --> 00:00:13,646
So let's fix that, with a CDN.

6
00:00:15,556 --> 00:00:17,428
Here is the problem.

7
00:00:17,588 --> 00:00:22,063
A message between Mumbai and Virginia takes somewhere between a hundred eighty

8
00:00:22,063 --> 00:00:24,644
and two hundred fifty milliseconds, each way.

9
00:00:24,804 --> 00:00:28,183
Add the TCP and TLS handshakes, and the first byte of a

10
00:00:28,183 --> 00:00:30,948
cold connection can take four to six hundred.

11
00:00:31,108 --> 00:00:34,726
And your one server has to hold every connection, and send every

12
00:00:34,726 --> 00:00:35,404
byte itself.

13
00:00:36,014 --> 00:00:39,326
That is too slow, and too much work for one machine!

14
00:00:40,436 --> 00:00:44,060
So here is the idea. Think of a chain of warehouses.

15
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:48,420
The factory is your origin server. Do not ship everything from there.

16
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:52,708
Put a regional warehouse in the middle. That is the origin shield.

17
00:00:52,868 --> 00:00:56,045
And a local one right in Mumbai. That is a pop, a

18
00:00:56,045 --> 00:00:57,212
point of presence.

19
00:00:57,372 --> 00:01:00,036
Now the shopper orders from the local shelf.

20
00:01:00,196 --> 00:01:03,652
The factory only ships when every warehouse is empty.

21
00:01:04,762 --> 00:01:07,402
Four words, and you can follow everything else.

22
00:01:07,562 --> 00:01:11,570
A hit: the pop has a fresh copy, and answers instantly.

23
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:15,522
A miss: no copy, so it asks the next warehouse up.

24
00:01:15,682 --> 00:01:20,193
TTL: how long a copy counts as fresh. Max age equals thirty

25
00:01:20,193 --> 00:01:22,258
six hundred means one hour.

26
00:01:22,418 --> 00:01:26,426
And a pop is simply one CDN data center, in one city.

27
00:01:27,536 --> 00:01:30,872
How does she reach the nearest pop? There are two ways.

28
00:01:31,032 --> 00:01:35,943
Anycast. Every pop announces the same IP address, and internet routing simply

29
00:01:35,943 --> 00:01:40,152
sends her to the closest one. Cloudflare and Fastly work this way.

30
00:01:40,312 --> 00:01:44,871
GeoDNS. The CDN's name servers hand out a different IP address for

31
00:01:44,871 --> 00:01:49,983
each region, based on where the resolver is. CloudFront and classic Akamai

32
00:01:49,983 --> 00:01:51,088
setups use this.

33
00:01:52,198 --> 00:01:54,454
Now she asks for the product photo.

34
00:01:54,614 --> 00:01:58,622
If the copy is fresh, that is a hit. Served in milliseconds.

35
00:01:58,782 --> 00:02:01,825
If it is stale but has an ETag, the pop asks the

36
00:02:01,825 --> 00:02:04,614
origin one cheap question: has this changed?

37
00:02:04,774 --> 00:02:08,278
A three oh four reply means no. Reuse what you have.

38
00:02:08,438 --> 00:02:11,525
And if there is no copy at all? That is a miss.

39
00:02:11,525 --> 00:02:13,430
Fetch it, store it, serve it.

40
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:18,092
Let us look at real headers from MDN, served by Fastly.

41
00:02:18,252 --> 00:02:21,372
Cache control says: keep this for an hour.

42
00:02:21,532 --> 00:02:25,060
Now look at age. Two thousand two hundred five.

43
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:28,855
That response had been sitting in a cache for over half an

44
00:02:28,855 --> 00:02:31,676
hour. The origin never even saw this request!

45
00:02:31,836 --> 00:02:35,659
And x cache lists one result per tier it passed through: miss,

46
00:02:35,659 --> 00:02:36,276
hit, miss.

47
00:02:37,386 --> 00:02:39,834
Headers decide what a CDN may keep.

48
00:02:39,994 --> 00:02:43,930
Max age: fresh for this many seconds, in every cache.

49
00:02:44,090 --> 00:02:48,284
S max age: the same, but for shared caches only, like the

50
00:02:48,284 --> 00:02:48,578
CDN.

51
00:02:48,738 --> 00:02:52,453
No cache. It sounds like don't cache, but it is not! It

52
00:02:52,453 --> 00:02:56,370
means: cache it, but check with the origin before serving.

53
00:02:56,530 --> 00:02:58,666
No store is the real never store.

54
00:02:58,826 --> 00:03:02,258
And private means only the user's own browser may keep it.

55
00:03:03,368 --> 00:03:05,240
Next, the cache key.

56
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:09,439
By default it is the scheme, host, path and query string. Same

57
00:03:09,439 --> 00:03:10,416
key, same copy.

58
00:03:10,576 --> 00:03:14,917
Varying on Accept Encoding is fine. Gzip and brotli just get their

59
00:03:14,917 --> 00:03:15,640
own copies.

60
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:19,948
But vary on Cookie? Almost every request has a different cookie, so

61
00:03:19,948 --> 00:03:24,344
almost every request becomes its own key, and your hit ratio collapses.

62
00:03:24,504 --> 00:03:28,442
And strip tracking parameters like u t m source, or one page

63
00:03:28,442 --> 00:03:29,952
turns into many misses.

64
00:03:31,062 --> 00:03:34,470
Picture a popular photo whose copy has just expired.

65
00:03:34,630 --> 00:03:39,211
A hundred requests arrive within five milliseconds, from pops all over. Every

66
00:03:39,211 --> 00:03:41,590
one of them misses, at the same instant.

67
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:46,599
Without protection, all hundred go to your origin. That is a stampede,

68
00:03:46,599 --> 00:03:48,296
and you caused it yourself.

69
00:03:48,456 --> 00:03:53,594
Request collapsing fixes it. The first request goes. The others wait for

70
00:03:53,594 --> 00:03:53,808
it.

71
00:03:53,968 --> 00:03:56,632
The origin shield does the same, one tier up.

72
00:03:56,792 --> 00:04:00,800
A hundred requests. Exactly one origin request.

73
00:04:01,910 --> 00:04:04,022
How do you get rid of an old copy?

74
00:04:04,182 --> 00:04:08,928
The best answer is: do not purge at all. Use versioned URLs.

75
00:04:08,928 --> 00:04:12,725
A new deploy means a new URL, and the old one is

76
00:04:12,725 --> 00:04:14,070
simply abandoned.

77
00:04:14,230 --> 00:04:17,806
Tag purge removes only the pages tagged with one object.

78
00:04:17,966 --> 00:04:21,888
Soft purge marks copies stale, but keeps serving them while a fresh

79
00:04:21,888 --> 00:04:22,766
one is fetched.

80
00:04:22,926 --> 00:04:27,478
And purge everything? Instant, but every pop hits your origin at once.

81
00:04:27,478 --> 00:04:30,534
That is how a routine deploy becomes an outage.

82
00:04:31,644 --> 00:04:33,804
Now, what must never be cached?

83
00:04:33,964 --> 00:04:38,884
Anything with Set Cookie. Mark authenticated responses private or no store.

84
00:04:39,044 --> 00:04:43,388
For paid downloads or video, use signed URLs, checked at the edge.

85
00:04:43,548 --> 00:04:48,182
Then two attacks to know. Cache poisoning: an unkeyed header lets one

86
00:04:48,182 --> 00:04:50,532
attacker response reach every user.

87
00:04:50,692 --> 00:04:55,660
Cache deception: add slash nonexistent dot CSS to a private page, and

88
00:04:55,660 --> 00:04:57,172
the CDN may cache it.

89
00:04:57,332 --> 00:05:01,642
The fix for both: the cache key must include everything the response

90
00:05:01,642 --> 00:05:02,276
varies on.

91
00:05:03,386 --> 00:05:07,901
Not everything can be cached. Checkout, or a logged in dashboard, must

92
00:05:07,901 --> 00:05:10,610
reach the origin. But the CDN still helps.

93
00:05:10,770 --> 00:05:14,790
TLS and TCP end at the nearby pop, so the slow handshakes

94
00:05:14,790 --> 00:05:16,482
happen over a short hop.

95
00:05:16,642 --> 00:05:20,986
The pop keeps warm connections open to your origin, and reuses them.

96
00:05:21,146 --> 00:05:25,551
HTTP 3 avoids head of line blocking, and survives a switch from

97
00:05:25,551 --> 00:05:26,810
Wi-Fi to cellular.

98
00:05:26,970 --> 00:05:31,050
Some providers even carry the long leg over their own private backbone.

99
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,544
Then there is edge compute: your own code, running at the pop.

100
00:05:35,704 --> 00:05:39,088
A request first passes the WAF and bot checks.

101
00:05:39,248 --> 00:05:41,120
Then the cache.

102
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:43,416
And if needed, your edge function.

103
00:05:43,576 --> 00:05:48,306
Good jobs for the edge: a JWT signature and expiry check, A

104
00:05:48,306 --> 00:05:51,352
B buckets, redirects, header rewrites.

105
00:05:51,512 --> 00:05:55,563
But the real authorization decision stays at the origin, because the edge

106
00:05:55,563 --> 00:05:58,448
only has an eventually consistent view of the world.

107
00:05:59,558 --> 00:06:02,630
So, is a CDN always the answer? No.

108
00:06:02,790 --> 00:06:05,929
It adds a layer that can fail, or serve stale data. And

109
00:06:05,929 --> 00:06:09,126
a bug in Brazil means checking a pop you do not control.

110
00:06:09,286 --> 00:06:12,286
It fixes aggregate traffic, not one hot key.

111
00:06:12,446 --> 00:06:15,806
Highly personalized responses get little from caching.

112
00:06:15,966 --> 00:06:19,575
And for an internal service with no spread out users, a plain

113
00:06:19,575 --> 00:06:21,054
load balancer is simpler.

114
00:06:22,164 --> 00:06:24,036
Let us recap.

115
00:06:24,196 --> 00:06:27,628
A CDN keeps copies in pops close to your users.

116
00:06:27,788 --> 00:06:29,660
Headers say what it may keep.

117
00:06:29,820 --> 00:06:31,908
The cache key says what is shared.

118
00:06:32,068 --> 00:06:34,852
Collapsing turns a hundred misses into one.

119
00:06:35,012 --> 00:06:39,428
Prefer versioned URLs over purges, and never cache private data.

120
00:06:39,588 --> 00:06:43,476
Cheap checks at the edge. Real decisions at the origin.
